\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The weighted four-cycle Laplacian}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Use the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), with vertices
#{0,1,2,3}, edges #{e_i:i\to i+1}, and positive real weights
#{(1,t,1,t)}. For a field #{f=(a,b,c,d)}, the incidence values are
#{Df=(b-a,c-b,d-c,a-d)}. The
[counting adjoint](fdip-0007) sends an edge field to
##{D^*(u_0,u_1,u_2,u_3)
  =(u_3-u_0,u_0-u_1,u_1-u_2,u_2-u_3).}
First multiply by #{W_t=\operatorname{diag}(1,t,1,t)}, then apply
#{D^*}. The resulting #{L_t=D^*W_tD} has coordinate matrix
##{L_t=\begin{pmatrix}
1+t&-1&0&-t\\
-1&1+t&-t&0\\
0&-t&1+t&-1\\
-t&0&-1&1+t
\end{pmatrix}.}}
\p{For example, its first coordinate is
#{t(a-d)-(b-a)=(1+t)a-b-td}; the other coordinates follow from
the same return formula. Thus
##{L_t\delta_0=(1+t,-1,0,-t),\qquad L_t\mathbf1=0,}
and the counting energy is
##{\langle f,L_tf\rangle
=(b-a)^2+t(c-b)^2+(d-c)^2+t(a-d)^2.}
The counting adjoint #{D^*} does not depend on #{t}. The weighted
return map #{D^*W_t}, and hence #{L_t}, do. When #{t=1} the two
return maps coincide; otherwise the factors #{t} must be retained.}
\p{This calculation substitutes the four-cycle data into
\ref{fdip-0007}; its incidence convention follows
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}.
The [cyclic energy factorization](fdip-0014) gives the corresponding
operator identity.}
